WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.461
Authentication establishes who or what the
caller is.

2
00:00:06.541 --> 00:00:13.020
APIs commonly use API keys, bearer tokens,
OAuth 2.0 flows,

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:21.912
OpenID Connect identity tokens, service
accounts, workload identities, mTLS,

4
00:00:21.992 --> 00:00:26.917
or platform-specific identity mechanisms.

5
00:00:26.997 --> 00:00:35.290
The right pattern depends on the caller,
the trust model, and the operation.

6
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.424
A browser user, a mobile app, a partner
server,

7
00:00:40.504 --> 00:00:49.575
and an internal workload should not
automatically share the same identity design.

8
00:00:49.655 --> 00:00:56.005
API keys are simple, but simplicity comes
with tradeoffs.

9
00:00:56.085 --> 00:01:05.156
They often identify an application or
integration rather than an individual user,

10
00:01:05.236 --> 00:01:13.141
and they can become long-lived shared
secrets if not managed carefully.

11
00:01:13.221 --> 00:01:20.737
Bearer tokens are powerful because
possession is enough to use them.

12
00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:29.240
That means token storage, transport,
logging, lifetime, audience, issuer,

13
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.300
and revocation all matter.

14
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:39.119
A token copied into a log or artifact may
become an access path.

15
00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:45.678
OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect are often
discussed together,

16
00:01:45.758 --> 00:01:50.942
but they solve related and different
problems.

17
00:01:51.022 --> 00:01:57.112
OAuth 2.0 is commonly used for delegated
access to APIs.

18
00:01:57.192 --> 00:02:06.393
OpenID Connect adds an identity layer that
helps clients understand who the user is.

19
00:02:06.473 --> 00:02:15.026
In API design, teams should be clear about
which token type they are accepting,

20
00:02:15.106 --> 00:02:22.362
what audience it was issued for, who
issued it, how long it is valid,

21
00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.682
and which claims are trusted.

22
00:02:25.762 --> 00:02:31.723
Authentication is only the beginning of
API security.

23
00:02:31.803 --> 00:02:39.967
A caller can be real and still not be
allowed to perform a particular action.

24
00:02:40.047 --> 00:02:47.174
A service account can be valid and still
have too much permission.

25
00:02:47.254 --> 00:02:53.993
A token can be signed and still be
intended for a different API.

26
00:02:54.073 --> 00:03:00.941
Good identity handling narrows the
question to who is calling.

27
00:03:01.021 --> 00:03:10.351
Authorization, validation, and business
rules decide what that caller may actually

28
00:03:10.431 --> 00:03:10.820
do.
